NNAAMMEE
     llddaappccttll — control the LDAP daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llddaappccttll [--vv] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--rr _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y] [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llddaappccttll program controls the ldapd(8) daemon.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Use _f_i_l_e as the configuration file, instead of the default
             _/_e_t_c_/_l_d_a_p_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --rr _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Store and read database files in _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, instead of the de‐
             fault _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_l_d_a_p.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use _s_o_c_k_e_t instead of the default _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_l_d_a_p_d_._s_o_c_k to communi‐
             cate with ldapd(8).

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.

     The commands are as follows:

     ssttaattss   Show statistics counters.

     lloogg bbrriieeff
             Disable verbose debug logging.

     lloogg vveerrbboossee
             Enable verbose debug logging.

     ccoommppaacctt
             Compact all databases.  ldapd(8) does not have to be running.
             When compaction of a database file is complete, a special marker
             is appended to the database file that tells ldapd(8) to reopen
             the file and perform new requests against the compacted database.
             A write transaction is opened to force other processes to buffer
             write requests while performing compaction.  Read requests are
             handled without disruption.

     iinnddeexx   Re-index all databases.  ldapd(8) does not have to be running.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/run/ldapd.sock         default llddaappccttll control socket
     /etc/ldapd.conf             default ldapd(8) configuration file

